{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 1, :comments ["<b>Come, eat of my bread and drink of my wine I have mingled (Proverbs 9:5).</b>" "<b>Solomon</b> the king, peace be upon him, likened the Torah in this verse (Proverbs 9) to bread and wine, because bread and wine are the principal nourishment of the body; so too the Torah and wisdom are the principal nourishment of the soul. And he used the expression לכו ('go') and did not say בואו ('come'), to indicate that a person must go after wisdom and after those who learn it, in the manner of what is written (Proverbs 13:20): ״הולך את חכמים יחכם״ — 'he who walks with the wise becomes wise.' It should have said 'he who sits with the wise,' and he mentioned הולך ('going') to make clear that one should always go after them and subordinate himself to them, and thereby become wise. Or the sense of לכו may be that one must go even to a distant land to learn Torah, and this is what it says: לכו לחמו בלחמי — go to a distant land to eat my bread. And thus it says (Isaiah 55:3): ״הטו אזנכם ולכו אלי״ — 'incline your ear and go to Me'; it does not say 'come to Me,' because a person must go far away to hear words of Torah. He says: לכו לחמו בלחמי — go, one to another, you who seek to eat my bread; he warns them about the trait of jealousy, that they should remove it from their hearts, and that none of the sages should exercise dominion over himself so as not to go to his fellow who is his equal — needless to say if he is greater than he in wisdom — for through companionship wisdom grows and understanding increases. And thus our Sages, of blessed memory, said (Jeremiah 50:36): ״חרב אל הבדים ונואלו״ — 'a sword upon the liars, and they shall become fools' — a sword upon Torah scholars who sit alone each one by himself and engage in Torah; and not only that, but they become stupid, as it is written here ונואלו, and it is written there (Numbers 12:11): ״אשר נואלנו״. And ״שתו ביין מסכתי״ — 'drink of my wine I have mingled': the letter ב in לחמי is extra, and likewise the letter ב in יין, like its parallel (Genesis 41:2): ״ותרעינה באחו״. And it is known that diluted wine is beneficial and good for a person's body, and it also arouses the powers of the soul; for when it is undiluted, it surely harms body and soul, and therefore he used the expression מסכתי ('I have mingled'). And the parable comes to refer to the Torah and the commandments, all of which are for the benefit of body and soul. And further, this parable also includes another benefit, to explain and say: if a person will exert himself and strive for the nourishment of his body and go after it to a distant land, how much more so must he exert himself and strive even more for the nourishment of his soul, for there is no doubt that he will attain it after exertion, in the manner that Solomon said (Proverbs 2:4–5): ״אם תבקשנה ככסף וכמטמונים תחפשנה אז תבין יראת ה׳״ — 'if you seek her as silver and search for her as hidden treasure, then you will understand the fear of YHWH.' And every single commandment is nourishment for the soul; through it the soul is nourished by the radiance of the Shekhinah in the World to Come. And if a person can fulfill and perform the commandment in his own place, the reward is double; but if a person rises from his place to go after it, his reward will be complete, because he has in hand both the reward of going and the reward of doing. And this is what we find in the alacrity of Israel, as it is written (Exodus 12:28): ״וילכו ויעשו״ — 'and they went and did.' And thus we find in Moses, that he went to Israel from the camp of Levi to the camp of Israel to comfort them regarding his death, and regarding the greatness of Joshua, that he would be in his stead and fill his place. He did not wish to speak to them while they were all assembled and standing before him, but waited until each had gone to his tent, so that he could go to them — and this is what is written." "<b>And Moses went and spoke these words to all Israel. I am one hundred and twenty years old today.</b> It is possible to say that his days were 120 years, corresponding to the 120 days he stood on the mountain: forty days the first time to receive the tablets, forty days the second time to pray over the sin of the calf, forty the third time to receive the second tablets. And this is the reason for אנכי — and he did not say אני. And our Sages, of blessed memory, expounded: היום מלאו ימי ושנותי — 'today my days and years are fulfilled'; היום נולדתי והיום אמות — 'today I was born and today I die.' And thus he said at the beginning of his words (Deuteronomy 29:9): ״אתם נצבים היום״ — 'you are standing today'; the entire matter is joined, and all of it was on the day of his death. And this day was the seventh of Adar, and it was the end of the fortieth year; on that very day Moses died, on that very day he was born. On that very day Moses died — this is what is written at the end of the Torah (Deuteronomy 34:8): ״ויבכו בני ישראל את משה בערבות מואב שלשים יום ויתמו ימי בכי אבל משה״ — 'and the children of Israel wept for Moses in the plains of Moab thirty days, and the days of weeping and mourning for Moses were completed.' And it is fitting to join to this verse at the end of the Torah what is written at the beginning of Joshua (Joshua 1:11): ״ויצו יהושע את שוטרי העם הכינו לכם צדה כי בעוד שלשת ימים אתם עוברים את הירדן״ — 'and Joshua commanded the officers of the people: prepare provisions for yourselves, for in three days you will cross the Jordan.' <b>And whoever</b> joins these verses together will find the matter clear: that Joshua did not command to prepare provisions until the days of weeping and mourning for Moses were completed; therefore Scripture needed to say ויתמו ימי בכי אבל משה. And Scripture mentions there (Joshua 4:19): ״והעם עלו מן הירדן בעשור לחדש הראשון״ — 'and the people came up from the Jordan on the tenth of the first month.' And if you count backward from the tenth of Nisan thirty-three days — which are three days for provisions and thirty for the mourning of Moses — you will find the day of his death on the seventh of Adar. On that very day Moses was born — as it is written (Exodus 2:3): ״ולא יכלה עוד הצפינו״ — 'and she could no longer hide him.' This was on the sixth of Sivan, the day on which he was destined to receive the Torah, on which day an immense light was added to hint at his greatness on that day; and therefore she could no longer hide him, and she devised a plan to place him in a chest of reeds in order to conceal his body because of the intensity of his light. And if you count backward from the sixth of Sivan three months, you will find the day of his birth on the seventh of Adar — and therefore he said ירחים ('months'), which are the months of the moon, and they are Adar, Nisan, Iyar: two deficient and one full. And he did not say חדשים, because a חדש is a single division of the twelve in a year, and they are neither added to nor diminished; whereas the months of the moon are added to and diminished, and because of this he used the term ירחים, so that from this the day of the prophet's birth would be made clear to us. And behold, this is clear from Scripture: on the seventh of Adar Moses was born and on that very day he died. And this is what our Sages, of blessed memory, expounded: the Holy One, blessed be He, fills the years of the righteous from day to day, as it is said (Exodus 23:26): ״את מספר ימיך אמלא״." "<b>I can no more go out and come in.</b> In war, as I have done until now in the war against Sihon and Og. And the Ramban, of blessed memory, explained that he said this to comfort them regarding his death — for surely his strength then was as his strength now, and so Scripture testifies that even at the hour of his death his eye did not dim and his natural force did not abate — but he wished to strengthen their hearts and inform them that Joshua his minister would fill his place and cause them to inherit the land. And our Sages, of blessed memory, expounded: לצאת ולבא — in matters of Torah, teaching that the wellsprings of wisdom were stopped up from him. And behold, this was a miraculous act, so that he would not worry and his grief over death would not grow; and for another reason as well: so that Joshua would assume the authority and the kingship during Moses's lifetime."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 31, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:52:57.456064028Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}